## How This Works — A Collaborative Walkthrough

I'd like to walk through this carefully so everyone on the team feels comfortable with the approach. Please share any concerns — all perspectives are valued here.

### `allSubstrings(_ inputString:)`

| Step | What Happens | Why It Matters |
|------|-------------|----------------|
| Convert to `Array` | Swift strings use complex indexing, so we convert to a character array | Makes the team's (loop's) work manageable |
| Outer loop `i` | Marks the **start** of each substring | Each starting point opens new possibilities |
| Inner loop `j` | Marks the **end** of each substring | Paired with `i`, they define every slice |
| `String(characters[i..<j])` | Extracts the actual substring | Brings the piece together into something usable |

---

### `longestSubstring(_ strings:, k:)`

| Step | Collective Purpose |
|------|-------------------|
| `guard !strings.isEmpty` | Respectfully exits early if there's nothing to work with |
| Nested loops | Reviews every substring from every string — no contribution overlooked |
| `.filter { $0.contains(substr) }.count` | Measures how broadly shared a substring is across the group |
| `count >= k` | Ensures the substring meets the *group's minimum acceptance threshold* |
| `substr.count > longestStr.count` | Only elevates a new candidate if it genuinely improves upon what the group already has |

---

### Key Swift Differences Worth Noting Collectively

- **`guard` instead of `if len == 0`** — Swift's idiomatic, graceful early exit
- **`Array(string)`** — Needed because Swift's `String` indexing is not integer-based like Python
- **`.filter{}.count`** — Swift's elegant equivalent of Python's `sum(x in y for ...)`
- **`String?` (Optional)** — Swift's type-safe way of expressing "this might not exist," aligned with Python's `Optional[str]`

